Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.908
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:07.410
STEPHANIE WONG: Container
Threat Detection, KTD,

00:00:07.410 --> 00:00:09.780
is a built-in service in
Security Command Center

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:10.890
Premium.

00:00:10.890 --> 00:00:13.710
It can detect the most common
container runtime attacks

00:00:13.710 --> 00:00:16.320
and provide alerts in
Security Command Center,

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:18.780
and optionally in Cloud Logging.

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:20.310
Container Threat
Detection includes

00:00:20.310 --> 00:00:25.500
multiple detection capabilities,
an analysis tool, and an API.

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:27.430
To enable Container
Threat Detection,

00:00:27.430 --> 00:00:29.460
first make sure you have
Security Command Center

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:31.710
admin and Security
Center sources

00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:36.420
admin IAM rules, along with
the SCC Premium subscription.

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:39.840
On the SCC homepage,
go to the Sources page.

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:42.790
If you're a premium Security
Command Center subscriber,

00:00:42.790 --> 00:00:45.270
you can click the toggle next
to Container Threat Detection

00:00:45.270 --> 00:00:46.860
to enable it.

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:48.792
In your Cloud SCC
dashboard, you will

00:00:48.792 --> 00:00:50.250
find the Container
Threat Detection

00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:51.960
card listing current findings.

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:54.570
In our environment,
there is one finding.

00:00:54.570 --> 00:00:57.510
Some examples of the types of
findings that KTD can surface

00:00:57.510 --> 00:01:00.270
include added binary executions.

00:01:00.270 --> 00:01:02.038
This finding triggers
when a binary

00:01:02.038 --> 00:01:04.080
that was not part of the
original container image

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:05.459
was executed.

00:01:05.459 --> 00:01:08.490
If an added binary is
executed, it's a possible sign

00:01:08.490 --> 00:01:10.920
that an attacker has
control of the workload,

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:13.200
and they are executing
unwanted software

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:16.590
such as malware or
cryptocurrency mining software.

00:01:16.590 --> 00:01:18.090
Added binary loaded.

00:01:18.090 --> 00:01:19.738
This finding triggers
when a library

00:01:19.738 --> 00:01:21.780
that was not part of the
original container image

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:22.860
was loaded.

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:25.560
If an added library is
loaded, it's a possible sign

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:27.900
that an attacker has
control of the workload,

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:30.450
and they are executing
arbitrary code.

00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:31.710
Reverse shell.

00:01:31.710 --> 00:01:33.360
This finding triggers
when a process

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:37.230
started with stream redirection
to a remote-connected socket.

00:01:37.230 --> 00:01:38.760
An attacker can
use reverse shell

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:40.200
to communicate
from a compromised

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:42.810
workload to an
attacker-controlled machine.

00:01:42.810 --> 00:01:44.550
With this capability,
the attacker

00:01:44.550 --> 00:01:47.350
can control the workload to
perform malicious actions,

00:01:47.350 --> 00:01:49.557
for example as part of a botnet.

00:01:49.557 --> 00:01:52.140
You can learn more about these
detections in the documentation

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:53.550
link below.

00:01:53.550 --> 00:01:56.040
When Container Threat
Detection generates findings,

00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:58.290
you can view them in
Security Command Center,

00:01:58.290 --> 00:02:01.050
or in Cloud Logging, if you
have configured Security Command

00:02:01.050 --> 00:02:05.190
Centers Syncs to write to
Google Cloud's Operation Suite.

00:02:05.190 --> 00:02:07.410
To produce sample
findings for us to act on,

00:02:07.410 --> 00:02:10.289
we've set up a cluster on
Kubernetes Engine in a project

00:02:10.289 --> 00:02:12.450
within our organization.

00:02:12.450 --> 00:02:15.180
Using Cloud Shell, you can
trigger an added binary

00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:18.090
executed finding by dropping
a binary in our container

00:02:18.090 --> 00:02:19.740
and executing it.

00:02:19.740 --> 00:02:22.000
Once we access the
Cluster Control Plane,

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:24.900
we can deploy the latest
Ubuntu 18.04 image,

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:29.130
copy /bin/ls to another
location, and then execute it.

00:02:29.130 --> 00:02:31.110
You can trigger a
reverse shell finding,

00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:34.110
by deploying the latest
Ubuntu 18.04 image

00:02:34.110 --> 00:02:36.090
and copying in a
library that was not

00:02:36.090 --> 00:02:38.430
part of the original
container image.

00:02:38.430 --> 00:02:42.020
This example deploys the latest
Ubuntu 18.04 image, copies

00:02:42.020 --> 00:02:45.790
the library, libcso6,
to another location,

00:02:45.790 --> 00:02:47.850
and then loads it using ld.

00:02:47.850 --> 00:02:50.100
You can also trigger a
reverse shell finding

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:52.950
by starting a binary with
standard input redirection

00:02:52.950 --> 00:02:55.320
to a TCP-connected socket.

00:02:55.320 --> 00:02:58.770
This example starts /bin/echo
with redirection to the Google

00:02:58.770 --> 00:03:03.150
public DNS 8.8.8.8
on the DNS port.

00:03:03.150 --> 00:03:05.760
You can find these
commands in the link below.

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:08.115
To view findings, navigate
to the Threats Dashboard

00:03:08.115 --> 00:03:10.110
in Security Command Center.

00:03:10.110 --> 00:03:12.750
Our findings are listed
as critical threats.

00:03:12.750 --> 00:03:15.030
Drilling in, you can
find detailed information

00:03:15.030 --> 00:03:18.030
on the Source Properties tab
about the source container,

00:03:18.030 --> 00:03:23.520
including environment
variables, process arguments,

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:26.310
and Kubernetes labels.

00:03:26.310 --> 00:03:28.680
You can share this finding
with your team and remediate

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:30.450
by heading to the source.

00:03:30.450 --> 00:03:31.470
Congratulations.

00:03:31.470 --> 00:03:34.650
You've just completed setting
up Container Threat Detection.

00:03:34.650 --> 00:03:36.450
You can now detect
and be alerted

00:03:36.450 --> 00:03:38.670
to the most common
container runtime attacks

00:03:38.670 --> 00:03:41.790
from the Google Cloud console
or Cloud Audit Logging.

00:03:41.790 --> 00:03:43.940
[MUSIC PLAYING]

